        <section id="defensive-programming-example" >
          
            <h2>Defensive Programming example</h2>
              <p>Let’s say in the program on the previous page, we wanted to ensure that the two inputs to our function were always both integers, and never floating point numbers. In this scenario, we could use defensive programming to write in checks to see if this occurs, and act accordingly when it does.</p>

              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-python"><code class="language-python"><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token triple-quoted-string string">""" Python code with exception handling """</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Here we define a function to add two input values then print the answer.</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token keyword">def</span> <span class="token function">add_values_and_print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>a<span class="token punctuation">,</span> b<span class="token punctuation">)</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token comment"># Here, we defensively program to ensure numbers are Ints with 'if' statements</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">if</span> <span class="token builtin">isinstance</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>a<span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token builtin">float</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token operator">==</span> <span class="token boolean">True</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">"Input 'a' was Float type not Int. Converted to Int."</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    a <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token builtin">int</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>a<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">if</span> <span class="token builtin">isinstance</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>b<span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token builtin">float</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span> <span class="token operator">==</span> <span class="token boolean">True</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">"Input 'b' was Float type not Int. Converted to Int."</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    b <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token builtin">int</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>b<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">try</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span>				<span class="token comment"># This is where we try the operation</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    c <span class="token operator">=</span> a <span class="token operator">+</span> b</span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">except</span> TypeError<span class="token punctuation">:</span>	<span class="token comment"># This is where we state what to do (the exception) in case of a TypeError</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'TypeError. Please ensure that both input variables are Ints.'</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">return</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">else</span><span class="token punctuation">:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    c <span class="token operator">=</span> <span class="token builtin">int</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>c<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">    <span class="token keyword">print</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token string">'The output is equal to: '</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> c<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">  <span class="token keyword">return</span><span class="token punctuation">(</span>c<span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Now we test the function. First with integers:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">test_1 <span class="token operator">=</span> add_values_and_print<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token number">4</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Now with an integer and a string:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">test_2 <span class="token operator">=</span> add_values_and_print<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token string">'goat'</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line"><span class="token comment"># Now with an integer and a float:</span></span><br><span class="highlight-line">test_3 <span class="token operator">=</span> add_values_and_print<span class="token punctuation">(</span><span class="token number">2</span><span class="token punctuation">,</span> <span class="token number">3.7</span><span class="token punctuation">)</span></span></code></pre>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="result" >
          
            <h2>Result</h2>
              
              <div class="c-code"><pre class="language-text"><code class="language-text"><span class="highlight-line">The output is equal to:  6</span><br><span class="highlight-line">TypeError. Please ensure that both input variables are Ints.</span><br><span class="highlight-line">Input 'b' was Float type not Int. Converted to Int.</span><br><span class="highlight-line">The output is equal to:  5</span><br><span class="highlight-line"></span><br><span class="highlight-line">[Finished in 134ms]</span></code></pre>
</div>

            
              <p>Now, test_1 and test_2 still act as expected. However, using the ‘if’ statements starting on lines 7 and 11, whenever inputs ‘a’ and ‘b’ are 'floating point numbers', and if they are, print a message to the console to inform the user that they are converted to 'Ints'.</p>
<p>Using defensive programming, we can minimize the occurrence of bugs and errors, and make sure that we deal with them appropriately when they do occur.</p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
